          "source_text_sha256": "7b556cd723968acd6d6e70e5d2e9e5e7c65bdbd29be52b484423bd4e5e91815a",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Description: A witness reports an observation made on March 2, 2014 at 7:40 PM concerning multiple white or yellow-orange lights in the sky. The witness, traveling by car to continue their observation, will record a video and take photographs with their smartphone. Only one testimony about this phenomenon was collected. The witness sees several stationary bright points in the sky, the closest being blue-orange. One of them, higher than the others, disappears behind the clouds. That day, Infoclimat records note a light north wind, shifting to south around 7 PM in Lyon, and around 9 PM in Valence. It is difficult to determine what wind was blowing at 7:20 PM in Clonas, located between the two stations. See Bourg-Les-Valence weather and Lyon weather. The photos and videos are very similar to the Thai lantern photos and videos. The GEIPAN classifies this case as \"B\": probable observation of Thai lanterns.",
